the final shield wall has been formed  the last longship launched heroes have  risen  fallen and converted to christ the best  thing we can say about the final ten  episodes of michael hurst's genre  defining historical epic vikings  is that it went out fighting with a  sword in its hand  in some sense the last act is a  schizophrenic episode  it principally follows two groups led by  ragnar's surviving sons  as they march toward diametrically  opposed but equally inevitable  conclusions  ube's crew who spent much of the half  season floating on the vast and  uncharted atlantic ocean with neither  food nor  water is now settled in the golden land  of what's clearly north america  where they've discovered both a helpful  native american tribe  and an old friend floki the boat builder  after much  suffering at sea it appears ube and his  crew might finally get the chance to  live as farmers in a bountiful land with  new neighbors who know nothing of the  viking way  but when nad kills wagey two after  breaking  into his teepee in search of gold the  fragile peace between the two peoples is  threatened  on the other side of the sea ivar once  again challenges  alfred of wessex after king harold is  killed in battle  ivar assumes that the peace-loving  christian saxons  will have had enough of war under the  flag of truce  he and vitzerk offer alfred a typical  viking bargain  an exchange of hostages and land carved  out of wessex for the vikings to settle  which in turn would buy a temporary end  to the hostilities  steeled by his wife's advice alfred  refuses the offer  determined to purge the pagan horde from  his land once and for all  you are a byword for terror all over  this world  you do not see or feel the pity of  ordinary mortals  though ivar has the numbers he sees the  writing on the wall  he knows that he will die if he fights  the saxons again  and witzerk identifies that his  brother's eyes have turned blue  an apparent sign that ivar is about to  hurt himself  ivar does indeed perish in the battle  with alfred  he goes down screaming the same  challenge he first uttered in york  during the revenge raid for ragnar's  killing  when a young saxon soldier approaches  him with a knife ivarn odds  practically inviting the young nameless  christian to send him to valhalla  he dies in vitzerk's arms and witserk is  captured by the west saxons  he converts to christianity in exchange  for his life and takes the name  ethelstam a welcome nod to the  northumbrian priest  and friend of ragnar who perished all  those seasons ago  unlike ivar who embraces the viking way  to his last breath  ube renounces the brutality of  scandinavian law  after announcing his plans to cut the  blood eagle into nad as punishment for  his crime against the native americans  ube decides to instead show mercy oh now  that's not for you my friend  let me put you out of your misery  [Applause]  at the last moment with both his crew  and the tribe looking on  he abandons the barbaric ceremony and  provides nad with a quick  clean death it's an interesting  counterpoint to an  earlier blood eagle that ragnar elected  to complete on yarl borg  after the half-mad rival leader  attempted to have ragnar deposed  the dichotomy between the two sons of  ragnar couldn't be clearer at this point  and it serves hearst's apparent thesis  in the concluding season of his show  ube abandons the old ways and is  rewarded with the opportunity to walk  into a new age in a new land ivar clings  to the old ways and ultimately dies with  them  so what does it all mean in some sense  the two dueling narratives of the  vikings finale provide a meta commentary  on the violent viking culture  as well as a eulogy for its golden  heyday in the 9th century  as jarring as it often is lurching from  frenetic battle scenes in wessex to  idyllic landscapes in ube's golden land  the contrast provides important insight  into ube  and ivar who both ultimately come to  represent the two sides of their famous  father  like ube ragnar was a thinker and  explorer who wanted to expand the edges  of the map  and like to think of himself as a farmer  he was fascinated by the christian god  even though he never completely embraced  a conversion this site of ragnar lives  on inube  the eldest son of al slug is the  cerebral leader to ivar's pure viking id  like ivar ragnar could also be wild  vengeful and cruel  he was always a better raider than king  and gained most of his fame by pillaging  wessex and frankia  he never got to be a farmer and he  ultimately sacrificed himself as a means  to inspire his sons to take up  arms in wessex he perpetuated the cycle  of violence  even when he and king ekbert might have  found an accord born of an  obvious mutual respect ivar meets the  same end as his father on the same saxon  soil  [Music]  when ube tells otere he finally found  what ragnar was searching for  he's right though he misses the fact  that ragnar abandoned that search  in favor of war ube finds it because he  makes the choices that ragnar didn't  in the final moments of the episode and  the series we intercut between  scenes of ingrid ruling steadily in  katagat  and the resolution in wessex with both  eric and harold out of the way  ingrid appears to have solidified her  control of norway  she receives word of ivar and harold's  deaths and vitzerk's conversion  and she seizes the moment to eulogize  the deaths of the great viking heroes  the final scene features ube and floki  gazing  out at the sea from under native  american blankets for such a violent  series  the choice to end on a quiet moment of  camaraderie between two men who forsook  the viking way was a brave one though it  ultimately pays off  providing a satisfying bit of pathos and  a deep breath after six seasons of utter  brutality  is that the end it also may have been  intended to set up michael hurst's  forthcoming sequel series  vikings valhalla set to take place a  century later  and follow the next generation of viking  explorers to hunt for ube's golden land  so while we all look forward to that  voyage let us take a moment to lift a  horn of ale and say skull to a truly  singular series check out one of our  newest videos right here  plus even more looper videos about your  favorite movies and tv shows are coming  soon  subscribe to our youtube channel and hit  the bell so you don't miss a single one
 

